Filter function
Another important function from the dplyr package is filter(). This function only keeps rows that meet certain conditions. Try it out below. Pay attention to how sites and carapace size are excluded based on the conditions specified in the code. Note that I have selected only the date, site, latitude, size, and air_temp columns for the before data set, to help it fit onto the page.

viewof site_filt = Inputs.select(site_options_filt, {label: "Site"})







viewof min_size_filt = Inputs.select(size_options_filt, {label: "Minimum size"})







rows_filt = transpose(choices_filt)
match_filt = rows_filt.find(d =&gt; d.site === site_filt && d.size === min_size_filt)
















{
  const pre = document.createElement("pre");
  pre.className = "plot-code";
  const code = document.createElement("code");
  code.textContent = match_filt.code;
  pre.appendChild(code);
  return pre;
}







{
  const wrap = document.createElement("div");
  wrap.className = "side-by-side";
  wrap.innerHTML =
    "&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Before&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" + match_filt.before + "&lt;/div&gt;" +
    "&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;After&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" + match_filt.after + "&lt;/div&gt;";
  return wrap;
}












Note



The output is only showing how the filter() function changes the output using only the first 15 rows of data as input.


For our final analysis, we will want to keep all of our crab observations, so there is no use for the filter() function. However, there are other situations in data analysis where it could be beneficial. For example, we could use the filter() function to see if there are trends in crab carapace size at a single sampling site. See an example of this, combined with the summarise() function below.


Summarise function
To better understand the summarise() function, we will filter the data to a specific site and calculate some summary statistics about that site.

viewof site_fsum = Inputs.select(site_options_fsum, {label: "Site"})







viewof stats_fsum = Inputs.checkbox(summary_options_fsum, {label: "Summaries"})







rows_fsum = transpose(choices_fsum)
tables_fsum = transpose(site_tables)
match_fsum = rows_fsum.find(d =&gt; d.site === site_fsum && d.stats === stats_fsum.join("|"))
before_fsum = tables_fsum.find(d =&gt; d.site === site_fsum)






























{
  const pre = document.createElement("pre");
  pre.className = "plot-code";
  const code = document.createElement("code");
  code.textContent = match_fsum.code;
  pre.appendChild(code);
  return pre;
}







{
  const wrap = document.createElement("div");
  wrap.className = "side-by-side";
  wrap.innerHTML =
    "&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Before:&lt;/b&gt; filtered rows (first 10 of " + before_fsum.n + ")&lt;/p&gt;" + before_fsum.before + "&lt;/div&gt;" +
    "&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;After&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" + match_fsum.after + "&lt;/div&gt;";
  return wrap;
}






As mentioned above, if we want to see if crabs at more northern sites are larger, we will want to keep all observations from all the different sites. So instead of filtering by site, we will utilise the group_by() and the summarise() functions compare across sampling locations. Since these samples are taken from multiple sites across a latitudinal gradient, the group by function will help us keep track of the different sites. Test out what happens when you group by site versus when you don’t!

viewof group_gsum = Inputs.select(group_options_gsum, {label: "Group by"})







viewof stats_gsum = Inputs.checkbox(summary_options_gsum, {label: "Summaries"})







rows_gsum = transpose(choices_gsum)
match_gsum = rows_gsum.find(d =&gt; d.group === group_gsum && d.stats === stats_gsum.join("|"))
















{
  const pre = document.createElement("pre");
  pre.className = "plot-code";
  const code = document.createElement("code");
  code.textContent = match_gsum.code;
  pre.appendChild(code);
  return pre;
}







{
  const div = document.createElement("div");
  div.innerHTML = match_gsum.table;
  return div;
}






We can see that using the group_by() function is important to see the trend across the sampling sites. Without it, the parameters are summarised across ALL the data, and we can’t compare the different sites to each other.



Plotting Our Data
However, summary statistics only give us a numerical was of seeing the trends. An easy way to quickly see trends in data is to use plotting. Creating plots allows for readers to quickly understand the relationships between variables, without having to compute or sort parameters.
Here we will use the ggplot2 package to visualize the raw data and site means.

Geoms: point and jitter
geom_point() draws one dot per row of your data and places points exactly on their appropriate x and y coordinate, such that if points overlap, you can only see the top one.
geom_jitter() on the other hand, considers the overlapping of points. Using this function instead of geom_point() will place points slightly offset of one another so that individual points are more easily visualized.


Smooth lines
geom_smooth() is a function for ggplots that fits a trend line and confidence band to your data.
There are many available methods that geom_smooth() can use to fit a line to your data, but the most commonly used ones are “lm”, “loess”, and “gam”.
The “lm” option will fit a straight line linear regression to your data.
The “loess” option is the default for geom_smooth(), so if you don’t specify a method it will use “loess” when your dataset has less than 1,000 points. This method fits a curve that follows the local structure of the data.
The “gam” option is best for large data sets (&gt;1,000 observations), which fits a smooth spline.


Themes
But what’s the point of making a plot if you can’t make it pretty! In ggplot2 there are nine built-in themes that control the non-data display elements of your figure. These nine themes are:

theme_grey()
theme_bw()
theme_linedraw()
theme_light()
theme_dark()
theme_minimal()
theme_classic()
theme_void()
theme_test()



Putting it all together
Since our examples before did not create a new named object, I am going to start with the select() and summarise() functions to summarise the data, so that I can use these data in my figure.
I have used the same functions as above, but instead of just running the dplyr functions on the data set, I have created a new named object where I ran these functions.

crab_summary &lt;- crab_data %&gt;% 
  select(site, size, latitude) %&gt;% 
  group_by(site) %&gt;% 
  summarise(mean_size = mean(size), 
            latitude = unique(latitude),
            sd_size = sd(size))

After creating this summary data, I can use both the original and summary data sets to create a beautiful plot.








Figure 2: Fiddler crab carapace size (mm) in relation to latitude. Light grey points represent individual observations, while black larger points show mean values for sample sites. Smooth shows positive relationship between carapace size and latitude.





Using the dropdowns, see if you can create the code by changing the options with the dropdowns. There is only one combination of dropdowns that will create a plot identical to mine above.
Pay close attention to which functions and calls change when you select different drop down options.

viewof geom_sel = Inputs.select(geom_options, {label: "Geom"})







viewof smooth_sel = Inputs.select(smooth_options, {label: "Smooths"})







viewof theme_sel = Inputs.select(theme_options, {label: "Themes"})







rows_plot = transpose(plot_choices)
match_plot = rows_plot.find(d =&gt; 
d.data_geom === geom_sel && d.smooths === smooth_sel && d.themes === theme_sel)
















{
const img = document.createElement("img");
img.src = match_plot.image;
img.style.maxWidth = "100%";
return img;
}






The code that made this plot:

{
const pre = document.createElement("pre");
pre.className = "plot-code";
const code = document.createElement("code");
code.textContent = match_plot ? match_plot.code : "";
pre.appendChild(code);
return pre;
}












Tip



An alternative to creating a separate data set is the use the stat_summary() instead of summarise() + geom_point()
For example:

ggplot(data = crab_data, 
       aes(x = latitude, y = size)) + 
  geom_jitter(color = "grey50", alpha = 0.5) + 
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "point",
               color = 'grey30', size = 3) +
  labs(x = "Latitude", y = "Carapace size (mm)") 

Note how geom_point(crab_summary, ...) was replaced with `stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = “point”) but the mean points would still be the same!


Now that you have created a plot, we can see that body size does indeed increase with latitude. This shows that the data follows the predictions posed by Bergmann’s Rule.



Conclusion
You also now know how to conduct a simple analysis using the dplyr and ggplot2 functions. The beauty of tidyverse packages in R is that they follow an English type grammar structure, so instead of thinking purely in code, you can think about what you want to do to your data and translate that into functions.
A full workflow for this kind of analysis is shared here
We can plot our mean sizes alongside the site label on our map to see that as we go further north, crabs become larger








